r="#0f172a">Functional Status:</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;Current status: Bedrest.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;BADLs: Dependent (mRS: 5)</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">[Recovery of Neurological/Motor Function]</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- 05/06–05/07: Severe right hemiplegia. MMT 2/2, Brunnstrom Stage III on the right side.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- 05/07–05/11: Rapid motor recovery noted. Right upper extremity progressed from Brunnstrom III to IV-V (elbow can flex/extend against gravity, slight shoulder and finger movements).</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- 05/11–05/18: Lower extremity motor function progressed to Brunnstrom stage IV. Voluntary motor control is actively recovering in the subacute phase.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">[Training Summary]</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">PT:</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Status: Progressed to Brunnstrom Stage IV for RUE (proximal/distal) and RLE.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Functional Mobility: Bed mobility (rolling to right/left) ranges from independent to minimal assistance. Bridging exercises performed under supervision. Supine-to-sit transfers require moderate assistance.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Balance &amp; Standing: Static sitting balance is Fair. Able to stand for 2 minutes with minA and one hand held.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">OT (05/11):</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Motor: RUE Brunnstrom IV-V (proximal) / IV (distal). Slight right finger flexion/extension, elbow flexion/extension against gravity, slight shoulder flexion.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Cognitive/Perceptual: Attention impaired (+), memory impaired, orientation impaired.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Occupational Performance: Bed mobility requires moderate assistance; feeding requires total assistance.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">ST (05/15):</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Swallowing Training: Tested with an IDDSI Level 4 CVA test meal using a spoon (1 spoon per bite, total 250g). Fully consumed.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp;- Progress: Oral Transit Time + Pharyngeal Transit Time (OTT+PTT) was 1-2 seconds. Wet voice count: 0. Only 1 episode of post-swallow choking occurred. Patient reported tasting milk in her throat.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">Goal: Daughter: quadricane walk</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">---</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">### Physiatrist Clinical Analysis &amp; Missing Information</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">#### Differential Diagnoses &amp; Clinical Impressions</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">1.&nbsp; **Acute Ischemic Stroke, Left Medial Pontine (Confirmed):** Explains the right-sided hemiplegia, facial palsy, dysarthria, and dysphagia (due to disruption of corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts).</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">2.&nbsp; **Stroke Evolution vs. Inadequate Antiplatelet Response:** The patient experienced a "stuttering" onset—initially presenting on 05/05, discharging after transient improvement, then worsening severely on 05/06. This pattern is classic for basilar branch disease or atherothrombotic pontine infarction.&nbsp;</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">3.&nbsp; **Vascular Cognitive Impairment (VCI):** Although initial consult noted "cognition intact," the subsequent formal OT assessment on 05/11 revealed clear deficits in attention, memory, and orientation. This could